# The Required rule of the same name holds every other wordy phrase.
# These sit here, because each also has a literal sense the short form
# does not carry. `a number of` counts something (`Read returns a
# number of bytes`), `in terms of` names a unit (`the cost is
# expressed in terms of bytes`), and `with respect to` names a variable
# (`the derivative with respect to x`). `on the order of` stays out,
# because it also names a sequence (`depend on the order of the
# members`).
#
# It hit 124 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 17 of 20 sampled hits
# read better in the short form. The 3 misses counted something (`a
# number of seconds`, `a number of rows`), and the message tells the
# writer to keep those.
expr main [(a number of), (in terms of), (with respect to), (in relation to), (in light of), (by means of), (in such a way that), (in a manner that)]
let message "Wordy. Write the short form, such as `several` for `a number of` or `relative to` for `with respect to`. Keep the phrase when it names an exact count or a unit, as in `a number of seconds`."
let description "Flags `a number of`, `in terms of`, `with respect to`, and similar phrases that most sentences can replace with one word."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Many flags take a value."
allows "The limit counts bytes."
